{{also|copout|cop-out}}
==English==

===Etymology===
Attested since 1942 as “flee, escape, {{l|en|cop}} (an) out”.<ref>{{cite-book|author=Lester V. Berrey|coauthors=Melvin van den Bark|title=The American Thesaurus of Slang|year=1942}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-au-cop out.ogg|a=AU}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|*}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} To [[avoid]] or [[shirk]], either by failing to perform, or by performing in a grossly [[insufficient]], [[negligent]], or [[superficial]] manner.
#: {{ux|en|Faced with the prospect of cooking for himself, his first thought was to '''cop out''' and order a pizza.}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=2000|author=w:Bill Oddie|title=Gripping Yarns|page=67|passage=There was no bird fair at Druridge so I didn't have to feel guilty about '''copping out''' of it.}}
# To [[plead]] [[guilty]] and ask for [[mercy]].
# {{lb|en|idiomatic|slang}} To blame; to assign blame to; to get into trouble.
#: {{ux|en|His lawyer '''copped''' him '''out''' on just one felony charge.}}
# {{lb|en|idiomatic|dated|American}} To remove from a situation or crowd, such as for arrest or punishment.
#: {{ux|en|Somebody should have '''copped''' him '''out''' last night.}}
# To leave a gang.
#: {{ux|en|I can't believe you '''copped out''' on us.}}
# {{lb|en|idiomatic|slang}} To abandon, let down or betray (someone).
#: {{ux|en|She thought I'd '''copped out''' on her.}}
# {{lb|en|idiomatic|slang|transitive}} To [[win over|win]] (someone) over; to get on (someone's) good side.

====Synonyms====
* [[weasel out]]

